cabal-version:      3.12
name:               mithril-ir
version:            0.1.0.0
synopsis:           Host tool for the Mithril Core IR
description:
  Cabal package for Mithril's deterministic host tool.  Besides help
  and version output, the @mithril@ executable supports
  @mithril validate FILE@: JSON parsing, structural validation of a
  Mithril Core v0 document against the canonical @core/schema.json@
  — compiled into the tool at build time, so no runtime file or
  environment lookup selects the grammar — complete Core v0 name
  resolution: declaration-name uniqueness in every global and
  owner-local namespace, and resolution of every name reference in
  its correct namespace — complete Core v0 static typing of the
  resolved document: term, policy, effect, and result types, operand
  and relation-endpoint compatibility, enum-order permutation
  validity, CreateEntity initializer completeness, and guarantee
  well-typedness — and deterministic Core v0 normalization of the
  well-typed document into an explicit internal typed normalized
  representation: statically typed terms, materialized enum ranks,
  explicit endpoint bindings, declaration-ordered CreateEntity
  initializers, and explicit guarantee structure, with resolved
  identifiers preserved and names and source paths kept as metadata.
  @mithril contract FILE@ runs the same complete pipeline and then
  renders the normalized document as a deterministic, line-oriented,
  human-readable security contract — the first consumer of the typed
  normalized Core: declarations in normalized order, fully
  parenthesized constructor-preserving terms with their stored static
  types, materialized enum ranks, explicit endpoint and scope
  bindings, and the selected guarantees explicitly labelled
  unverified proof obligations.
  A successfully resolved document is carried as an explicit internal
  decoded and name-resolved Core representation (identifier-based
  references with JSON source paths; generic JSON ends at the
  internal decode boundary); the typechecker consumes exactly that
  representation, the normalizer consumes exactly the typed
  document, reusing the typechecker's judgment rather than rerunning
  type inference of its own, and the contract renderer consumes
  exactly the normalized document, repeating no earlier stage.
  Normalization is structural only — no boolean simplification,
  constant folding, operand reordering, policy evaluation, or
  semantic optimization, and no canonical equality between
  differently authored documents is claimed — and contract rendering
  is deterministic restatement only: it evaluates no policy, proves
  and checks nothing, and is not a semantic diff.
  @mithril verify FILE@ runs the same complete pipeline and then the
  first connected verifier slice: a pure deterministic support gate
  over the normalized document and, for exactly one supported
  obligation shape — a document selecting exactly one Acme-derived
  NoSelfPrivilegeEscalation obligation whose normalized structure
  corresponds to the mechanized safe changeRole proof rule of the
  Agda spike — a deterministically generated Agda obligation module,
  materialized together with trusted kernel modules embedded at
  compile time into a fresh isolated workspace and checked by
  exactly Agda 2.8.0 with --safe --no-libraries --ignore-interfaces.
  A supported document reports VERIFIED (exit 0); everything else is
  UNSUPPORTED (exit 3) with deterministic reasons — including the
  canonical Acme example, whose TenantIsolation and
  AuthenticatedMutation obligations remain unverified, documents
  selecting no or several guarantees, and semantically equivalent
  but differently authored shapes — and every checker problem is a
  tool failure (exit 2), never a semantic verdict: no violation
  outcome exists in this milestone.  The Haskell generator, the
  embedded Agda kernel, the external Agda toolchain, and the support
  rule itself remain trusted components.
  @mithril wasp generate CORE_FILE WASP_ROOT@ and @mithril wasp check
  CORE_FILE WASP_ROOT@ implement the first confined target slice, the
  Wasp Confinement Profile v0: after the same complete pipeline and
  the verify gate (VERIFIED required), the one shared
  NoSelfPrivilegeEscalation support plan the verifier consumed is
  lowered deterministically into a closed Wasp 0.25.0 application on
  PostgreSQL — main.wasp.ts, schema.prisma, the one authenticated
  Action declaration and its generated TypeScript implementation
  (Wasp authentication, context.user.id as the only identity,
  argument validation, authorization reads and the SetRelation write
  in one Prisma interactive transaction at Serializable isolation
  with a bounded deterministic P2034 retry, no raw SQL), the
  dependency, TypeScript, and Vite configuration, a minimal static
  client shell, a fixed-bytes ownership marker, and a deterministic
  manifest — fourteen managed files whose identifiers, filenames, and
  route are fixed role-derived target names, never authored names.
  generate stages the complete bundle beside the root, checks it,
  and installs it as a whole (an absent or empty root is initialized;
  an owned root is replaced by whole-directory renames with rollback;
  an unmarked nonempty root or any unmanaged path is refused without
  mutation), refuses root paths with dot, empty, or trailing-separator
  components, linked ancestors or roots, and an occupied backup
  sibling path (never touched), installs the root private (mode 0700
  whatever the umask), and finishes with the confinement check;
  check performs no writes, walks the complete source root without
  following symbolic links, rejects hard links, compares every
  managed file byte-for-byte with the regenerated bundle, and rejects
  missing, altered, and unexpected inputs (exit 4) with deterministic
  sorted diagnostics.  The authority is the regenerated closed path
  inventory plus exact bytes; the denylist scan only labels bypasses.
  The pure renderer attests supported-shape lowering only; VERIFIED
  provenance is stated by the command after the verifier reported it.
  Wasp, Node, Prisma, PostgreSQL, the templates, and the lowering
  remain trusted; no semantic-preservation theorem exists; the
  confinement claim covers the source snapshot at the time of
  checking, not concurrent same-user mutation after it, privileged
  users, checker or CI compromise, dependency compromise, external
  database-credential holders, or tampering after the Wasp build;
  this is not a general Wasp backend, a whole-product generator, a
  complete verifier, or a runtime sandbox.  No other compiler stage is
  implemented: no complete verifier, no policy evaluator, no semantic
  diff, no runtime enforcement, and no target generation beyond this
  one slice, and acceptance by validate, contract, or an unsupported
  verify neither verifies a model nor establishes any guarantee.
homepage:           https://github.com/mithril-ir/mithril
bug-reports:        https://github.com/mithril-ir/mithril/issues
license:            Apache-2.0
license-file:       LICENSE
author:             Mithril contributors
maintainer:         Mithril contributors
copyright:          2026 Mithril contributors
category:           Development
build-type:         Simple
tested-with:        GHC == 9.12.4
extra-doc-files:    README.md

-- core/schema.json is the canonical Core v0 grammar; it is embedded
-- into the library at compile time (Mithril.Core.Internal.BundledSchema),
-- never installed as package data and never looked up at run time, so
-- it belongs in the source distribution but not in data-files.  The
-- handwritten Acme example, the invalid test fixtures, the frozen
-- golden security contracts, and the API-boundary probe material
-- (the probe package, the driver, and the driver's own isolation
-- self-test) are consumed by the test suite and the documented probe
-- commands, so source distributions must carry them too.
-- The generated Wasp fixture under test/fixtures/wasp-acme is the
-- reviewed golden output of 'mithril wasp generate' for the supported
-- fixture (pinned byte-for-byte by the test suite and CI, regenerated
-- only through the tool, never hand-edited); the rename variants under
-- test/fixtures/wasp-renames are the supported documents whose renamed
-- declarations the test suite renders and the Wasp job compiles and
-- builds; and the four files under test/wasp-integration are the
-- test-only Wasp/PostgreSQL harness (its library, its self-tests, and
-- the HTTP battery) outside the confinement claim.  All belong in the
-- source distribution and not in data-files.
-- The five agda/Mithril kernel modules listed here are the trusted
-- Agda kernel sources embedded into the library at compile time
-- (Mithril.Core.Internal.AgdaKernel) for the verifier's isolated
-- checking workspaces; like the schema they are never installed as
-- package data and never read from the repository at run time, so
-- they belong in the source distribution but not in data-files.
extra-source-files:
    agda/Mithril/Base.agda
    agda/Mithril/Core.agda
    agda/Mithril/Effect.agda
    agda/Mithril/Guarantee.agda
    agda/Mithril/Policy.agda
    cabal.project
    cabal.project.probes
    core/schema.json
    examples/acme/acme.mir.json
    test/api-probes/internal/*.hs
    test/api-probes/probe/*.hs
    test/api-probes/probe/probe.cabal
    test/api-probes/run-api-probes.sh
    test/api-probes/test-run-api-probes.sh
    test/fixtures/acme-nspe-unsafe.mir.json
    test/fixtures/acme-nspe.mir.json
    test/fixtures/acme.contract.txt
    test/fixtures/coverage.mir.json
    test/fixtures/malformed.mir.json
    test/fixtures/near-core.mir.json
    test/fixtures/nspe.generated.agda
    test/fixtures/unknown-name.mir.json
    test/fixtures/wasp-acme/.gitignore
    test/fixtures/wasp-acme/.mithril-wasp-profile
    test/fixtures/wasp-acme/.npmrc
    test/fixtures/wasp-acme/.wasproot
    test/fixtures/wasp-acme/main.wasp.ts
    test/fixtures/wasp-acme/mithril.manifest.json
    test/fixtures/wasp-acme/package.json
    test/fixtures/wasp-acme/schema.prisma
    test/fixtures/wasp-acme/src/MainPage.tsx
    test/fixtures/wasp-acme/src/mithrilCaseAction.ts
    test/fixtures/wasp-acme/tsconfig.json
    test/fixtures/wasp-acme/tsconfig.src.json
    test/fixtures/wasp-acme/tsconfig.wasp.json
    test/fixtures/wasp-acme/vite.config.ts
    test/fixtures/wasp-renames/acronyms-digits.mir.json
    test/fixtures/wasp-renames/declaration-order.mir.json
    test/fixtures/wasp-renames/leading-characters.mir.json
    test/fixtures/wasp-renames/reserved-words.mir.json
    test/fixtures/wasp-renames/same-target-spelling.mir.json
    test/fixtures/wasp-renames/string-scope.mir.json
    test/fixtures/wasp-renames/wasp-auth-names.mir.json
    test/fixtures/welltyped.contract.txt
    test/fixtures/welltyped.mir.json
    test/wasp-integration/battery.mjs
    test/wasp-integration/lib.sh
    test/wasp-integration/run-wasp-integration.sh
    test/wasp-integration/test-harness.sh

source-repository head
  type:     git
  location: https://github.com/mithril-ir/mithril

-- 'cabal check' rejects an unconditional '-Werror' in a package
-- description, so the option sits behind this manual flag, which
-- cabal.project switches on for every local and CI build.
flag werror
  description: Treat GHC warnings as errors.
  manual:      True
  default:     False

-- Gates the expected-to-fail in-package non-coercion probe
-- executables at the end of this file; see the comment there.  Must
-- stay off (the default) for every canonical build.
flag internal-probes
  description: Build the in-package identifier non-coercion probes (expected-to-fail compilations driven by test/api-probes/run-api-probes.sh).
  manual:      True
  default:     False

common shared
  default-language: GHC2021
  build-depends:    base >= 4.21 && < 4.22
  ghc-options:
    -Wall
    -Wcompat
    -Widentities
    -Wincomplete-record-updates
    -Wincomplete-uni-patterns
    -Wmissing-export-lists
    -Wpartial-fields

  if flag(werror)
    ghc-options: -Werror

-- The stage machinery, the explicit decoded and name-resolved Core
-- representation (Document, Resolved, SourcePath, Syntax), the
-- shared static type language with the one statement of the
-- declared-type projections and the ordered-comparison shape
-- (StaticType), the explicit typed normalized representation
-- (Normalized), the one shared NoSelfPrivilegeEscalation support
-- gate and plan (NspeSupportPlan), the verifier slice (Verify), the
-- Wasp emitter (Wasp), the pure Wasp confinement checker
-- (WaspConfinement), and the effectful Wasp filesystem boundary
-- (WaspFilesystem) live in this
-- package-private named sublibrary.  That is an internal
-- testability and encapsulation mechanism, not a packaging or
-- distribution decision: visibility: private means no other package
-- can depend on it or import its modules — the downstream
-- compile-fail probes pin exactly that — while this package's own
-- test suite can inspect the real production models and the
-- in-package probes can prove the identifier namespaces
-- non-coercible.  Never make it public, never add it to a downstream
-- package's dependencies, and never re-export its modules from the
-- public library.
library core-internal
  import:          shared
  visibility:      private
  hs-source-dirs:  src-internal
  exposed-modules:
    Mithril.Core.Internal.Document
    Mithril.Core.Internal.Normalized
    Mithril.Core.Internal.NspeSupportPlan
    Mithril.Core.Internal.Resolved
    Mithril.Core.Internal.SourcePath
    Mithril.Core.Internal.StaticType
    Mithril.Core.Internal.Syntax
    Mithril.Core.Internal.Verify
    Mithril.Core.Internal.Wasp
    Mithril.Core.Internal.WaspConfinement
    Mithril.Core.Internal.WaspFilesystem

  -- directory, filepath, and unix serve exactly the Wasp filesystem
  -- boundary (Mithril.Core.Internal.WaspFilesystem): root-path
  -- validation with no-follow metadata, the link-count-aware
  -- snapshot walker, and the whole-directory installation swap.  The
  -- unix package is the explicit POSIX dependency of that boundary
  -- (Linux is the exercised platform).
  build-depends:
    , aeson >= 2.2 && < 2.3
    , bytestring >= 0.12 && < 0.13
    , containers >= 0.7 && < 0.8
    , directory >= 1.3 && < 1.4
    , filepath >= 1.5 && < 1.6
    , text >= 2.1 && < 2.2
    , unix >= 2.8 && < 2.9

library
  import:          shared
  hs-source-dirs:  src
  exposed-modules:
    Mithril.CLI
    Mithril.Command.Contract
    Mithril.Command.Validate
    Mithril.Command.Verify
    Mithril.Command.Wasp
    Mithril.Core.Contract
    Mithril.Core.Normalization
    Mithril.Core.Resolution
    Mithril.Core.Typing
    Mithril.Core.Validation
    Mithril.Core.Verification
    Mithril.Core.Wasp

  -- Mithril.Core.Internal.BundledSchema owns the compile-time-embedded
  -- bytes of core/schema.json; Report owns the frontend's diagnostic
  -- vocabulary; Decode owns the one JSON reading that produces the
  -- decoded representation; Typecheck owns the Core v0 static-typing
  -- judgment over that representation (and the shared type-query
  -- facility the normalizer consumes); Normalize owns the Core v0
  -- normalization pass from the typed model to the normalized one;
  -- Contract owns the deterministic security-contract rendering pass
  -- over the normalized model (behind Mithril.Core.Contract);
  -- Mithril.Command.Internal.Diagnostic owns the one
  -- diagnostic-escaping convention shared by the command boundaries.
  -- The representations themselves sit in the private core-internal
  -- sublibrary above.  None of these modules may ever move to
  -- exposed-modules (nor the sublibrary to public visibility):
  -- hiding them is what keeps external code from constructing
  -- documents, resolved nodes, or normalized nodes, minting or
  -- coercing identifiers, extracting the underlying payload,
  -- coercing one pipeline stage into another, reaching the
  -- typechecker's, normalizer's, or contract renderer's internals,
  -- or validating against anything but the canonical schema.
  -- Mithril.Core.Internal.AgdaKernel owns the compile-time-embedded
  -- bytes of the trusted Agda kernel modules, and
  -- Mithril.Core.Internal.AgdaChecker owns the one process boundary
  -- to the external Agda 2.8.0 checker (fresh isolated workspaces,
  -- version pinning, outcome classification); both stay hidden so no
  -- downstream package can read the embedded kernel or invoke the
  -- checker outside the staged pipeline.  The verifier's support
  -- gate, generator, and orchestration live in the private
  -- core-internal sublibrary (Mithril.Core.Internal.Verify), behind
  -- the public Mithril.Core.Verification boundary; the shared
  -- NoSelfPrivilegeEscalation support gate and plan both backends
  -- consume (Mithril.Core.Internal.NspeSupportPlan), the Wasp emitter
  -- (Mithril.Core.Internal.Wasp), the pure confinement checker
  -- (Mithril.Core.Internal.WaspConfinement), and the effectful Wasp
  -- filesystem boundary with its fault-injection seam
  -- (Mithril.Core.Internal.WaspFilesystem) live there too, behind the
  -- public Mithril.Core.Wasp boundary and the Mithril.Command.Wasp CLI
  -- boundary.
  other-modules:
    Mithril.Command.Internal.Diagnostic
    Mithril.Core.Internal.AgdaChecker
    Mithril.Core.Internal.AgdaKernel
    Mithril.Core.Internal.BundledSchema
    Mithril.Core.Internal.Contract
    Mithril.Core.Internal.Decode
    Mithril.Core.Internal.Normalize
    Mithril.Core.Internal.Report
    Mithril.Core.Internal.Typecheck

  -- template-haskell serves exactly two purposes: embedding
  -- core/schema.json into the library at compile time, so that no
  -- runtime package-data lookup (Paths_mithril_ir/mithril_ir_datadir)
  -- can substitute the grammar that mints the StructurallyValid
  -- stage, and embedding the trusted Agda kernel modules the same
  -- way, so no runtime lookup can substitute the kernel the verifier
  -- materializes.  directory, filepath, and process are the GHC boot
  -- libraries behind the verifier's production checker boundary
  -- only: fresh isolated workspaces and the external Agda 2.8.0
  -- process invocation.
  build-depends:
    , aeson >= 2.2 && < 2.3
    , bytestring >= 0.12 && < 0.13
    , containers >= 0.7 && < 0.8
    , directory >= 1.3 && < 1.4
    , filepath >= 1.5 && < 1.6
    , jsonschema >= 0.3.0.1 && < 0.4
    , mithril-ir:core-internal
    , process >= 1.6 && < 1.7
    , regex-tdfa >= 1.3.1 && < 1.4
    , template-haskell >= 2.23 && < 2.24
    , text >= 2.1 && < 2.2

executable mithril
  import:          shared
  hs-source-dirs:  app
  main-is:         Main.hs
  other-modules:   Paths_mithril_ir
  autogen-modules: Paths_mithril_ir
  build-depends:
    , mithril-ir
    , text >= 2.1 && < 2.2

test-suite mithril-ir-test
  import:          shared
  type:            exitcode-stdio-1.0
  hs-source-dirs:  test
  main-is:         Main.hs
  other-modules:
    Mithril.CLIProcessTests
    Mithril.CLITests
    Mithril.CoreContractTests
    Mithril.CoreModelTests
    Mithril.CoreNormalizationTests
    Mithril.CoreRepresentationTests
    Mithril.CoreResolutionTests
    Mithril.CoreTypingTests
    Mithril.CoreValidationTests
    Mithril.CoreVerificationTests
    Mithril.CoreWaspTests
    Mithril.NormalizedModelTests
    Mithril.Test
    Mithril.TestEnv

  -- process runs the actually-built mithril executable (provided on
  -- PATH by build-tool-depends) for the process-level CLI regression
  -- tests; directory and filepath let the schema-override and Wasp
  -- filesystem regressions build and reliably remove collision-safe
  -- scratch directories, and unix lets the Wasp confinement
  -- regressions create the hard links they must see rejected.  The private core-internal sublibrary is
  -- the deliberate white-box seam: it lets Mithril.CoreModelTests
  -- inspect the real resolved model produced by the public pipeline
  -- without exposing any internal module to code outside this
  -- package.  No test framework is used.
  build-depends:
    , aeson >= 2.2 && < 2.3
    , bytestring >= 0.12 && < 0.13
    , directory >= 1.3 && < 1.4
    , filepath >= 1.5 && < 1.6
    , mithril-ir
    , mithril-ir:core-internal
    , process >= 1.6 && < 1.7
    , text >= 2.1 && < 2.2
    , unix >= 2.8 && < 2.9
  build-tool-depends:
    mithril-ir:mithril

-- The in-package identifier non-coercion probes: executables of this
-- package (so they legitimately depend on the private core-internal
-- sublibrary and import the real internal identifier definitions),
-- driven by test/api-probes/run-api-probes.sh alongside the
-- downstream probes.  The control must compile; the two attack
-- executables must FAIL to compile — GHC refusing the cross-namespace
-- coercion is the point — so the manual internal-probes flag
-- (declared next to werror above) stays off for every canonical
-- build, and the probe script enables it with a solver constraint
-- inside its own build directory.
executable probe-internal-control
  import:          shared
  hs-source-dirs:  test/api-probes/internal
  main-is:         InternalControl.hs
  build-depends:   mithril-ir:core-internal

  if !flag(internal-probes)
    buildable: False

executable probe-internal-coerce-entity-enum
  import:          shared
  hs-source-dirs:  test/api-probes/internal
  main-is:         CoerceEntityEnum.hs
  build-depends:   mithril-ir:core-internal

  if !flag(internal-probes)
    buildable: False

executable probe-internal-coerce-relation-action
  import:          shared
  hs-source-dirs:  test/api-probes/internal
  main-is:         CoerceRelationAction.hs
  build-depends:   mithril-ir:core-internal

  if !flag(internal-probes)
    buildable: False
